# The align conformance scene: the cross-axis contract as an
# assertion, in the modes the classification cannot confuse with
# geometry luck.
#
# column@root (the root) carries align=stretch, ratified 2026-08-22: its
# two container children span the window's width, which no other mode
# produces from children whose naturals differ. Stretch geometry is
# DEGENERATE — a spanning child satisfies the start/center/end
# predicates too — so every classifier answers stretch first when all
# children span, and the positional modes classify only a container
# with at least one non-spanning child. The separability burden is the
# scene's, as always: these children hug far short of the window when
# the mode is absent.
#
# column@centered carries align=center: its children have deliberately
# different natural widths (a label, a button, and the row), so
# centers coinciding is CENTER and nothing else — starts differ, ends
# differ, extents differ from the content box. row#0 carries
# align=baseline with a label, a button, and a TALL image (2x64): the
# image has no text baseline, so its bottom sits ON the baseline (the
# CSS replaced-element rule), stretching the row's cross axis far past
# the text children — that construction is what separates the modes,
# because kaya's text controls alone share similar baseline-to-height
# ratios and a hug-height row collapses start/center/end/baseline
# inside the classification tolerance (measured on macOS: baseline
# placement equaled center exactly with a label beside an entry).
#
# row#1 hosts the ruling's own construction: column@fitcol is a NESTED
# container with grow=1 and align=stretch — the shape the portfolio
# dashboard's first photograph caught drawing a third of itself on
# macOS while every model observable stayed green (the GAP entry
# beside the dynamic-tables milestone). expect_fills column@fitcol holds
# the grow half (the container's box spans the track its weight
# earned) and expect_aligned holds the stretch half (its children span
# the box). Both were watched failing against the pre-ruling SwiftUI
# interpreter.
#
# start needs no scene: every other scene's geometry rides the
# normalized start default. end has a classification arm on every
# backend; the recordings are its visual record until a scene earns
# it.
expect label#0 "align probe"
expect_aligned column@root "stretch"
expect_aligned column@centered "center"
expect_aligned row#0 "baseline"
expect_aligned column@fitcol "stretch"
expect_fills column@fitcol
